course: dictionary
lesson: 6345
title: Mouthparts of insects
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A mouthpart is one of the parts around an insect's mouth that it uses to get food. Some mouthparts bite, some suck and some chew. The word itself gives a clue: mouth plus part means a piece of the mouth.
explain: Read the context: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts and lap up the blood.» Cut the skin and lap up the blood tell you the mouthparts are used to cut and to take in food. Blade-like means shaped like a sharp blade.
explain: Different insects have different mouthparts. Butterflies have a long sucking mouthpart, beetles have biting mouthparts, and mosquitoes have piercing ones. Check a new word by putting your meaning into the sentence: does «mosquitoes have piercing mouthparts» still make sense with "parts for getting food" in place of "mouthparts"? It does.
fact: A mouthpart is one of the parts around an insect's mouth.
fact: Insects use their mouthparts to bite, suck or chew food.
fact: Butterflies have a long sucking mouthpart.
fact: Beetles have biting mouthparts, and mosquitoes have piercing ones.
fact: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts», so their bite can hurt more than a mosquito's.
exercise: What is a mouthpart? | A tiny wing on a fly | The sound an insect makes when it eats | A part of an insect's leg used for walking | *One of the parts around an insect's mouth that it uses to bite, suck or chew food
exercise: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts and lap up the blood.» What do horseflies use their mouthparts for? | *To cut the skin and take in blood | To spin silk for a web | To make a loud buzzing sound | To carry pollen from flower to flower
exercise: Which insect has a long sucking mouthpart? | *Butterfly | Mosquito | Beetle | Horsefly
test: Which insect has biting mouthparts? | *Beetle | Mosquito | Butterfly
test: Mosquitoes have piercing mouthparts. What does piercing suggest? | *Sharp enough to go into skin | Soft and furry | Long and curly | Bright and colourful
exam: «Horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts, so the bite hurts more than a mosquito's.» Why does the horsefly bite hurt more? | Because mosquitoes cut the skin with blades | Because horseflies are bigger than butterflies | Because the blood is cold | *Because horseflies cut the skin with blade-like mouthparts
